TypeScript reports “Property does not exist on type” for some new browser APIs because its built-in DOM types only include APIs that at least two browser engines support. You can fix it by swapping in a fuller DOM library or by declaring the missing member yourself.


You call an API that MDN documents and Chrome already runs, and the compiler tells you it doesn’t exist. Typing it as` any` makes the red line go away, but you lose type checking on that code.


This article explains why TypeScript’s DOM types trail behind browsers, then covers two type-safe fixes and the runtime check you still need with either one.


## Key Takeaways


- TypeScript’s lib.dom.d.ts is generated by microsoft/TypeScript-DOM-lib-generator, which leaves out any API supported by fewer than two major browser engines.
- modern-web-types lowers that bar to one stable engine. Install it with` npm install --save-dev @typescript/lib-dom@npm:modern-web-types` .
- On TypeScript 6 or newer, the package only takes effect with` "libReplacement": true` in compilerOptions. TypeScript 4.5 through 5.x needs no extra setting.
- To add one missing member yourself, use` interface Window` in a global` .d.ts` file. A` type` alias will not merge.
- Having types for an API does not mean the browser supports it, so single-engine APIs still need a check like` 'fetchLater' in window` .


## What Does “Property Does Not Exist on Type” Mean?


TypeScript error TS2339, “Property does not exist on type”, appears when your code calls a real browser API that TypeScript’s bundled DOM types don’t declare. Your own code hasn’t added a global like` window.myThing` . The browser provides this method, but TypeScript’s types don’t know about it.


```text
window  .  fetchLater  (  '  /analytics  '  , {   method  :   '  POST  '  ,   activateAfter  :   60_000   });
// error TS2339: Property 'fetchLater' does not exist on type 'Window & typeof globalThis'.
```


[fetchLater()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Window/fetchLater) queues a request that the browser sends when the user leaves the page (it unloads or goes into the bfcache), or when the optional` activateAfter` timeout runs out, if that happens first. Philip Walton’s announcement of modern-web-types also uses element-scoped[view transitions](https://blog.openreplay.com/simple-introduction-view-transitions-api-browser/) and the Long Animation Frame` scripts` property as examples of APIs hit by the same error.


## Why Do TypeScript DOM Types Lag Behind Browsers?


TypeScript DOM types lag behind browsers on purpose. The[TypeScript-DOM-lib-generator README](https://github.com/microsoft/TypeScript-DOM-lib-generator#why-is-my-fancy-api-still-not-available-here) sets the bar for inclusion at two or more of the major engines, Gecko (Firefox), Blink (Chrome/Edge), and WebKit (Safari), so that only APIs with real cross-vendor agreement get in. Every Chromium-based browser runs on Blink, so they all count as one engine.


Here is what that means in practice. An API that has shipped only in Blink can run in Chrome, Edge, and every other Chromium browser, and TypeScript will still say it doesn’t exist. The[web-features entry for fetchLater](https://web-platform-dx.github.io/web-features-explorer/features/fetchlater) lists only Chromium-based browsers as supporting it, so it doesn’t pass the two-engine rule.


The “Property does not exist on type” error reflects TypeScript’s inclusion policy. It doesn’t tell you whether the browser running your code supports the API.


## Fix 1: Replace lib.dom With modern-web-types


[modern-web-types](https://github.com/philipwalton/modern-web-types) is a package by Philip Walton that swaps out TypeScript’s DOM and WebWorker libraries without other changes to your code. As his[announcement post](https://philipwalton.com/articles/modern-web-types/) explains, it is built with TypeScript’s own generator and w3c/webref data, but it includes any API that has shipped in one stable browser engine.


Install it under the` @typescript/lib-dom` alias. That is the name TypeScript looks for when it resolves its DOM library:


```text
npm   install   --save-dev   @typescript/lib-dom@npm:modern-web-types
```


On TypeScript 6 or newer, installing the alias does nothing until you also opt in to[lib replacement](https://www.typescriptlang.org/tsconfig/#libReplacement) :


```text
{
"  compilerOptions  "  : {
"  libReplacement  "  :   true
}
}
```


On TypeScript 4.5 through 5.x, leave this setting out. TypeScript 4.5 introduced the @typescript/lib-* lookup and applied it automatically. TypeScript 5.8 put it behind the` libReplacement` flag but left the flag on by default, and TypeScript 6 switched it off, as the[modern-web-types pull request recommending lib replacement](https://github.com/philipwalton/modern-web-types/pull/5) explains. According to the[TypeScript 7.0 announcement](https://devblogs.microsoft.com/typescript/announcing-typescript-7-0/) , 7.0 adopts the 6.0 defaults, including` libReplacement: false` , so 7.0 also needs the flag.


### Worker Projects


Worker code uses a separate entry point, added through` types` . Leave` "WebWorker"` out of` lib` , because the entry point replaces it:


```text
{
"  compilerOptions  "  : {
"  types  "  : [  "  modern-web-types/webworker  "  ],
"  lib  "  : [  "  ESNext  "  ]
}
}
```


On TypeScript 5.x, setting` types` at all stops TypeScript from auto-including every @types/* package in node_modules. On TypeScript 6 and newer,` types` already defaults to` \[\]` , so nothing is auto-included either way. In both cases, if you depend on packages such as` @types/node` , add them to the same array. The README also lists` serviceworker` ,` sharedworker` , and` audioworklet` entry points.


## Fix 2: Declare the Missing API With Interface Merging


If you only need one API, a small declaration file will do. Declare an interface with the same name as the built-in one, and TypeScript[merges the declarations](https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/declaration-merging.html) . It has to be an` interface` . A` type Window = { ... }` alias fails with a duplicate identifier error, because type aliases don’t merge.


The signature below follows MDN: fetchLater() accepts the same arguments as fetch() plus an` activateAfter` option, and it returns a FetchLaterResult whose` activated` boolean tells you whether the request has gone out yet.


```text
// src/types/fetch-later.d.ts
interface   DeferredRequestInit   extends   RequestInit   {
activateAfter  ?:   number  ;
}


interface   FetchLaterResult   {
readonly   activated  :   boolean  ;
}


interface   Window   {
fetchLater  (  input  :   RequestInfo   |   URL  ,   init  ?:   DeferredRequestInit  )  :   FetchLaterResult  ;
}
```


This works without a` declare global` wrapper because a` .d.ts` file with no top-level` import` or` export` is a global script. Three rules keep it working:


- **Keep the file non-module.** If you add a top-level` import` or` export` , the file becomes a module. The interfaces stop merging globally, and you would need` declare global` again.
- **Make sure tsconfig covers it.** The file has to match your` include` patterns.
- **Delete it when TypeScript ships the API.** Once lib.dom declares the same members, your copies are redundant at best and can conflict at worst.


This declaration types` window.fetchLater(...)` only. Calling the bare global` fetchLater(...)` also needs a global function declaration, so stick to` window.fetchLater` in your code.


## Do You Still Need Feature Detection?


Yes. TypeScript types describe what an API looks like. They don’t tell you which browsers have it. Both fixes make the compiler accept` fetchLater` , but browsers without it will still throw at runtime, so guard the call:


```text
if (  '  fetchLater  '   in   window  ) {
try   {
}   catch   {
// quota exceeded: fall back or drop the beacon
}
} else {
// fall back, e.g. send the data immediately
}
```


The` try` block is there because MDN recommends catching` QuotaExceededError` around nearly every fetchLater() call. Treat single-engine APIs as progressive enhancement, and check support against your own browser targets on MDN’s fetchLater page.


## Which Fix Should You Use?


Approach Setup Coverage When TypeScript catches up


modern-web-types One install, one flag on TS 6+ Every single-engine API Nothing to remove


Hand-written` .d.ts` One small file per API Only what you write Delete the file


` as any` /` @ts-ignore` None None Hides real errors indefinitely


Use modern-web-types if your project uses several new APIs or regularly adopts features before they reach other engines. Use a hand-written` .d.ts` if you need one or two members and would rather not replace the whole DOM library. Avoid` as any` and` @ts-ignore` : they turn off checking for the whole expression or line and will hide genuine mistakes later. Enforcing that with a[TypeScript ESLint setup](https://blog.openreplay.com/lint-typescript-eslint/) and the` @typescript-eslint/no-explicit-any` and` @typescript-eslint/ban-ts-comment` rules stops stray workarounds from building up.


## Conclusion


The TypeScript “Property does not exist on type” error for new browser APIs comes from TypeScript’s two-engine rule, not from a mistake in your code or a missing` "dom"` entry. Replace lib.dom with modern-web-types if you use new APIs regularly, or merge a single interface into` Window` for a one-off, and keep a runtime check around every call. Start by checking your TypeScript version: if it’s 6 or newer, add` "libReplacement": true` when you install the package.


## FAQs


Will installing @types/web fix missing types for new browser APIs?


Only for APIs that already meet the two-engine rule. @types/web is the same DOM library that ships inside TypeScript. It is generated by microsoft/TypeScript-DOM-lib-generator and published to npm, so it can deliver newer DOM types before the next TypeScript release. It applies the same inclusion policy, so a Chromium-only API like fetchLater stays missing. It installs through the same @typescript/lib-dom alias on TypeScript 4.5 and later, and on TypeScript 6 or newer it also needs libReplacement set to true.


Does adding DOM or ESNext to the lib option fix the error?


No. The lib option only chooses which of TypeScript's bundled declaration files get loaded. The bundled DOM file is generated under the two-engine rule, so adding 'DOM', 'DOM.Iterable', or 'ESNext' cannot add an API that file leaves out. ESNext adds JavaScript language features, not browser APIs. To fix the error, replace the DOM library with modern-web-types or declare the missing member in a global .d.ts file.


How do I declare a missing method on Element or HTMLElement instead of Window?


Merge into the interface that the specification defines the member on. In a global .d.ts file with no top-level import or export, declare interface Element with the new method. HTMLElement, SVGElement and the other element interfaces extend Element, so they all inherit it. Element-scoped startViewTransition() is defined on Element, so declaring it only on HTMLElement leaves SVG elements untyped. Take the exact signature from MDN or the spec.


